# Hashed, content-addressed build output — the filename changes whenever the
# content does, so it's safe to cache long-term. NOTE: this pattern overlaps
# the catch-all /* rule below for any chunk that actually exists in the
# current build. Cloudflare Pages/Sevalla merges (concatenates) Cache-Control
# values from every matching _headers block instead of letting the more
# specific rule win — confirmed live: a real chunk came back with BOTH this
# rule's and the catch-all's Cache-Control values joined by a comma. The
# catch-all deliberately includes a bare `no-cache` token for exactly this
# reason (see below), so the merge stays safe rather than producing an
# ambiguous "two different max-age values" header — real immutable chunks
# just cost one extra (cheap, conditional/304) revalidation per session
# instead of being served untouched from a year-long cache.
/_app/immutable/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Everything else — index.html, the SPA fallback _redirects resolves any
# unmatched/missing path to (client routes, or a previous deploy's now-
# deleted chunk path), the PWA service worker/manifest, static assets.
# `no-cache` (which still permits storage, it just forces revalidation
# before reuse) is what actually matters here, not the max-age numbers: on
# its own it always revalidates a path that only matches this rule, and if a
# path also matches the immutable rule above and Cloudflare merges the two
# values, `no-cache`'s revalidation requirement isn't something the other
# rule's max-age can silently override — the merged result still revalidates
# rather than being served stale for up to 30 days.
#
# That 30-day figure is Sevalla's/Cloudflare's own default shared-cache
# lifetime (s-maxage) for a static host response with no _headers override —
# it's what let the CDN edge keep serving a deploy-old index.html (with
# script tags pointing at chunk filenames the new deploy had already
# deleted) long after a new deploy shipped, which is what surfaced as
# "Failed to load module script ... MIME type text/html" in the browser.
/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
